    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Capitalism is an economic system based on private ownership of the means of production and its use for profit, with capital accumulation, competitive markets, commodification and wage labour.</li><li>It emerged in its modern form from agrarian change in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercantilism">mercantilism</a> of the 16th to 18th centuries, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> of the 18th century made it the dominant mode of production.</li><li>Largely unregulated in the 19th century, it was regulated through Keynesianism after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and freed again under neoliberalism from the 1980s; after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> it became a truly global system.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The word capitalism was coined by its critics: Louis Blanc used it in its modern sense in 1850 for “the appropriation of capital by some to the exclusion of others”, and in English it first appears, according to the Oxford English Dictionary, in Thackeray’s novel The Newcomes in 1854.</p></aside>
    </section>

